## Every execve inside a human login session, tagged so that vigil reads its own records and
## nobody else's.
##
##   auid>=1000 auid!=unset   only what a person started. Without them every exec of every
##                            service on this machine enters the stream, which is a volume
##                            nobody asked for. The cost is stated rather than hidden: what
##                            daemons and service accounts run is not seen here — that is what
##                            vigil's process collector is for.
##   arch=b64 and arch=b32    both, because a 32-bit process walks straight past a b64-only
##                            rule; it is the classic hole in an audit rule set. On a kernel
##                            with no 32-bit support the second line is refused and augenrules
##                            says so, which is why it is second.
##   -k vigil_exec            the tag. It is the whole of how vigil tells its records from
##                            yours, and it is why nothing here touches your rules.
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S execve -F auid>=1000 -F auid!=unset -k vigil_exec
-a always,exit -F arch=b32 -S execve -F auid>=1000 -F auid!=unset -k vigil_exec
